<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-classmanage2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Classroom Management, Part II</title>

<p>
  <alert>Pre-class activity</alert>: Read Section 2.3 (pages 15-24) in <em>Equitable and Engaging Mathematics Teaching: A Guide to Disrupting Hierarchies in the Classroom</em> by Daniel Reinholz, MAA Notes Volume #97, available here: <url href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_bGAXj1Rm6LOhaSvPJNEMuM_F9PkB9ec/view?usp=sharing"/>
</p>

<activity>
  <p>
    Discuss with your groups: what were 2-3 things that stood out to you in the reading? Why?
  </p>
</activity>

<activity>
<p>
  Discuss with your groups: Reinholz discusses in Section 2.3.1 the issue of <q>attention wandering</q>. How much does your attention wander during math courses?
  During other courses?
  Do you identify with your students who have trouble focusing during all of class, or do you have trouble understanding why this is difficult for them?
</p>
</activity>

<activity>
<p>
  Discuss with your groups: do you feel that your students' attention wanders during group work?
  Why do you think this happens?
  What are strategies you might use to help them return their focus after they get distracted?
</p>
</activity>

<p>
  In Section 2.3.2, Reinholz states that psychologists have found the following techniques to have different levels of effectiveness:
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        Highly effective: practice testing and distributed practice
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Moderately effective: interleaved practice, elaborative interrogation, and self-explanation
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Low effect: summarization, highlighting, keyword mnemonics, imagery use for text learning, re-reading
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
  Reinholz writes: 
  </p> 
  <blockquote>
    <p>
    "What is notable is that in these studies, the solutions to the practice problems need not be correct. 
  In other words, <em>imperfect</em> practice is still more beneficial for learning than studying a <em>perfectly</em> correct text. 
  In fact, some research suggests that the very act of incorrectly attempting a problem could have learning benefits in its own right."
</p>
  </blockquote>

<activity>
<p>
  Discuss with your groups: How are recitations in our department aligned or not aligned with these learning techniques?
</p>
</activity>

<activity>
<p>
  Discuss with your groups: How are your personal study/learning practices aligned or not aligned with these learning techniques?
</p>
</activity>

<p>
In Section 2.3.5, Reinholz discusses how active learning techniques can sometimes lead to an increase in inequitable outcomes between students in different demographic groups, but that there are effective strategies to avoid these inequitable outcomes.
He writes:
</p>

<blockquote>
<p>
"Educational theory and empirical data suggests that the inequities that arise in active learning classes can at least be partially attributed to the quality and quantity of participation opportunities afforded to different groups of students. 
In short, non-minoritized students take up more opportunities to participate, and thus learn, which mediates the production of inequity."
</p></blockquote>

<p>
  In her 2023 Joint Mathematics Meetings talk <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pRS9OMPfDRg"/>, Estella Johnson stated:
  
  </p> 
  <blockquote><p>
  "Thoughtful educators who are systematically trying to improve their teaching (by increasing student engagement during class time) are generally successful."
  </p></blockquote>
  <p>
  Thus, an important observation for novice teachers regarding using active learning in class is that attention must be given to ensuring that students feel welcome and that they belong in the class, so that they are comfortable fully engaging in the course activities.
  In a few weeks, we will devote an entire class meeting to the topic of creating supportive learning environments.
</p>

<p>
Reinholz writes:
<blockquote><p>
"In a lecture, the instructor does the hard work, making the content feel easy. 
An active classroom is much messier, and students struggle, make mistakes, and must work through repeated failures. 
Even though they are learning more through the process, it gives the (proper) impression that learning is hard work."
</p></blockquote>
</p>

<activity>
<p>
  Discuss this quote: Does this resonate with you, as a student or as a TA or as both or neither?
  When you consider the idea of a <q>messy</q> classroom as being a productive learning environment, does this sound like a contradiction to you, or does this sound coherent?
  Why?
</p>
</activity>






</chapter>